<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Keeping Our Communities Clean – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Keeping Our Communities Clean</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Who Will Bring the Cleaning Supplies?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: text: Simplify complex instructions for classmates. communication: Confirm comprehension of peer contributions during discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can simplify complex instructions by using a combination of writing, visuals, symbols, and drawings to show steps (e.g., write 'recycle,' draw a recycling symbol, and point to a picture of a recycling bin). Can confirm understanding by repeating peer contributions and adding visuals (e.g., writing 'clean desks' and showing a cloth, saying, 'We clean here.'). Can clarify peer ideas, using visuals and simple phrases (e.g., writes 'need gloves' and points to gloves to confirm understanding).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or drawings of cleaning supplies (e.g., broom, trash bag, gloves, cloth, recycling bin symbol). Students call out the names. The teacher asks, "What are these for?" and "Where can we use them?" linking to community cleanup. Students brainstorm what they remember about planning a school clean-up day.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows a simple instruction, like "Pick up trash," and demonstrates simplifying it by drawing a hand picking up trash next to the words. Then, the teacher models confirming understanding: "So, I must pick up trash, right?" using a questioning intonation and pointing to the drawing. The teacher also models clarifying: "We need gloves? (points to gloves) Like this?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'We should collect the recycling,' and draw a recycling symbol, what does 'should' mean? Is it a strong rule or a good idea?" (Good idea)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I repeat your idea and add a picture, what am I doing? Am I telling you something new or checking I understand?" (Checking understanding)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Keeping Our Communities Clean".</b> The teacher presents short, simple instructions for a clean-up task, then shows how to mediate them. For example, a written instruction: "Volunteers must wear gloves and bring a trash bag for collected items." The teacher shows a simplified version with visuals: writes 'gloves' + draws gloves, writes 'trash bag' + draws a bag. The teacher emphasizes the use of modal verbs (must, should, can) for instructions and plans, and future tense.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated instructions for a school clean-up day (e.g., a simple poster with tasks like "Collect trash," "Sort recycling," "Clean desks"), pre-prepared visual aids (symbols, simple drawings) that can be added to text. Use nouns: volunteers, trash, recycling, supplies, bags, gloves. Verbs: collect, sort, clean, bring. Modal verbs: must, can, should.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Participate in group discussions on community actions (Sociolinguistic). Describing plans and tasks: 'We will clean the classroom on Friday.' (Pragmatic).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students match simplified instructions (with visuals) to original, slightly more complex ones (e.g., "Volunteers must wear gloves" matches 'gloves' + drawing).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False: The teacher says a task like "We will clean the park" and shows a picture of cleaning desks. Students say if it's true or false for the picture shown.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Mediation.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Simplifying instructions using writing, visuals, symbols. Confirming comprehension using modal verbs (can, should, must), future tense, and imperatives. Vocabulary: community, supplies, trash, tasks, bags, recycling, clean, bring, pick up, collect, help, organize.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Simplify the Instruction):</b> The teacher provides sentences with cleaning tasks (e.g., "On Friday, we will pick up the trash in the schoolyard."). Students work in pairs to rewrite the instruction more simply, adding a simple drawing or symbol next to key words. (e.g., "Friday: pick up trash [draw bag]"). Teacher circulates, ensuring correct use of imperatives and future tense. Focus on word stress for 'pick up' and 'clean.'</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-controlled Practice (Confirm & Clarify):</b> Students are given a list of peer contributions (e.g., "Maria says we should bring water bottles," "Carlos thinks we can ask neighbors for help."). In pairs, one student repeats the contribution and tries to confirm it with a simple phrase and a visual/gesture (e.g., "So, we should bring water bottles? [mimics drinking]"). The other student confirms or clarifies. Example: "Yes, we MUST bring water bottles. For everyone."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction (Mediation Matching):</b> Students receive cards with complex instructions (e.g., "It is important that everyone collects their own litter and places it in the designated bins to keep our environment clean.") and other cards with simplified versions including a drawing (e.g., "Clean: pick up trash [drawing of trash in bin]"). They match the complex instruction to its simplified, mediated form. Then, they explain their match to their partner, confirming understanding: "This means we must pick up trash, right?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-20 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Keeping Our Communities Clean".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Poster Presentation and Peer Explanation. Students work in small groups (3-4 students) to finalize their "School Clean-Up Day Poster" (21st Century Skills Project). Each group creates a section of the poster with specific tasks, incorporating future tense ("We will clean..." "Everyone must help...") and time expressions ("on Friday," "next week"). Then, each group presents their poster section to another group. During the presentation, one student explains a task (e.g., "We will collect trash on the streets."). Another student from the presenting group must then simplify or clarify this instruction for the listening group, using writing, visuals, or gestures on a mini-whiteboard or scrap paper (e.g., writes 'collect trash' and draws a trash bag). The listening group must confirm their understanding by repeating the simplified instruction and adding their own gesture or simple phrase (e.g., "So, we collect trash? [pointing to bag]"). This ensures active mediation and comprehension checks.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in explaining tasks and plans, collaborative interaction within and between groups, successful mediation of information (simplifying, clarifying, confirming), use of target grammar (modal verbs, future tense, imperatives) and vocabulary. Pronunciation: emphasizing word stress on commands like 'bring,' 'clean,' 'sort.' Phonemic awareness: identifying /ɪ/ in 'bring' and /iː/ in 'clean' when peers speak.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during Stage 4, focusing on individual student contributions to mediation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will be assessed on their ability to: 1) Simplify an instruction from their group's poster using at least one visual/symbol and clear, simple language; 2) Confirm understanding of a peer's explanation by repeating it and adding a visual/gesture; 3) Clarify a peer's idea or question using simple phrases and visuals.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of commands and tasks.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'supplies,' 'bags,' 'clean,' 'recycle').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and conciseness when simplifying instructions (Mediation: text).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of visuals, symbols, and gestures to support mediation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to accurately repeat and confirm peer contributions (Mediation: communication).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and participation in group discussions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one instruction you simplified for your classmates today?" (Write it and draw a small picture.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did you confirm you understood your friend's idea?" (Circle: 'I repeated it' / 'I showed a picture' / 'Both')</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of explaining or understanding ideas with pictures was easy for you today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging when you tried to simplify instructions or confirm someone's idea?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this mediation lesson? The use of visual aids and symbols seemed effective for simplifying complex instructions for 6th graders. Students were engaged in the collaborative poster project. The repetition for confirmation worked well.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next time? Some students struggled to come up with appropriate symbols quickly; pre-made symbol cards could help. More practice on clarifying specific words rather than whole sentences might be beneficial. Ensure all students get a chance to mediate, not just present.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Keeping Our Communities Clean" theme. The next theme will introduce new vocabulary and scenarios, building on students' ability to work collaboratively and communicate ideas.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>